     1	# Chapter 23 — The First Quiet
     2	
     3	The haze had gone with the night, and in the morning of the three hundred and seventh the thing was there, across the plain, where the line said it would be, and he catalogued it from the plain's edge before anyone had spoken, because he had wanted to see it since a landing in the snow and the seeing should be entered before the wanting got to it.
     4	
     5	Low. That was the first fact, and the one he had not expected: a thing a half-day off across flat ground, and it did not stand up against the sky as the Span's crown had; it lay down. A wide grey lid on the plain, level, with a rim round it that broke the level — not a wall; a row of somethings, short and blunt, like teeth worn to the gum, going round all of it at the edge of what the eye could resolve. Nothing on it. Nothing over it. The plain went to it from every side without a hill or a fold, as if the ground had been worn down to meet it, and the sun came up behind it along the bearing and lit the rim first, and the lid was grey, and then the sun was over it and the whole thing was the colour the plain was, and would have been hard to see if you had not known where the line went.
     6	
     7	"A thousand," Karis said, beside him. She had not got the cord out. She had the map, and the straightedge from Ternhall along the line, and her eye, and she said the number as she said a number she was going to check. "Across. Two and a half of the Ring. Near enough. I'll know by dark."
     8	
     9	The rest was walking, and they went down onto the plain, eleven and a mule, in the order the plain took them, which was any order, because there was nothing on it to draw for.
    10	
