    37	She paced it along the line from outside, as she had paced the fourth, because that was how you paced a thing you had decided not to go into yet, and it took the afternoon, and he catalogued the afternoon by the count, because the count was the thing that was going to be entered.
    38	
    39	Lira on the line. She had the job from the Ring and the Stair and the fourth: walk the perimeter with her sigil lit, a stride at a time, until it went out, and step back until it came on, and call it. She did it as she had done it at four sites, and it went out at the stride, at a place a pace inside the stumps' inner faces, and she said "Gone," and stepped back, and said "Back," and Karis put a stone down, and they went on. Cord and stride and stone. Brom marking. Seln at the mule with the case, keeping the count of the plain, which was eleven and a mule and nothing else, because there was nothing else, because there was nothing on the plain to hide anything behind, and Seln, who had spent fifteen years using the ground, stood in the one place on the continent where there was no ground to use and did not like it and said so once, to the mule.
