    51	"The cuts. Every floor I've charcoaled, the cut's the same depth — a made thing's cut, the tool's width, one pass. These are that, and then they're worn, at the bottom, where nothing's touched them since, in a way the Ring's aren't and the Span's aren't and the Hall's aren't. The cut was made and then the stone went on being stone under it for longer than the others have been stone at all." She took the finger out. "And the layer under it. Look." She did not point; she put the straightedge from Ternhall flat against the stump's foot where the rock of the plain met it, and there was a line in the rock, a hair below the stump's base, that went round the stump and off across the plain's rock as far as the eye went — a stratum, a change in the grey, that the stump sat on and the floor sat on and everything else on the whole plain sat above. "That's under all of it. That's older than the floor. And the floor's sitting on it, cut to sit on it, the way the next layer's laid so the one above it fits. This is the first one." She said it in the voice, and then she said the rest, which was the part that was hers. "I'm not saying what it's the first of. I'm saying that by the strata this floor was cut before the others, and by the cuts these marks were made before the others, and that a thing that's written once and copied is written first somewhere, and this is where. The others are copies. This is nearer what they copied." She looked at the stump. "That's a finding about rock. It's not a finding about anything else."
