    71	He had wanted this before the middle, and had said so, and they had given it him: eleven people at the rim of the oldest quiet, a stride outside the stumps, in a row, as ten had stood inside an iron a pace apart at a Span; and he catalogued them at it, one each, because it was the count and the count was going to be the thing he left here when he walked away from it.
    72	
    73	The sigils went first. He watched them go, because a healer had said it was what she read for and a woman with a cord had said it was the finding she was afraid of what came after: every mark on every hand at the line going dark at the stride, at the same stride, at the same instant — Lira's, and Brom's, and the tall woman's and the broad man's and Teague's, and Vastin's, on a hand that had held a plane across a stream-bed the day before, and Karis's, on a hand that was bound, and Oryn's. The same. A hundred lamps. He did not say the rest of it and neither did she, and she looked at her own dark hand as she had looked at a column, and entered it, and drew nothing.
