     3	He counted the strides, because it was the only instrument he had left that read true in his hands, and because a man walking alone across the oldest floor on the continent should be doing something with his mouth that is not talking.
     4	
     5	Five hundred and six to the middle. He had the number from the cord's half, and he had it in the register he used for a price, and he walked it as he had walked a Span's arch and a Stair's terraces and a Ring's floor, on stone that had no seams and no give, with the last of the light lying along it from behind him, and his shadow going ahead of him toward the place, longer than he was, which was a thing he noted and did not make into anything.
     6	
     7	Ten. Twenty. The stumps behind him going from a rim to a row to a line, and the eleven at them going from people to a shape, and he did not look back, because he had said he would not and a thing said at a line is a thing kept. The thirteen rang in him faint as they rang at every rim — the ten that ran and the three that held, the bed and the plane and the line from heel to hand — as they had rung on four floors, doing nothing, because doing was not what a held thing did. The still place at the middle of them did what it did. And the thing under it, the thing that had turned at the line, turned with him: a stride at a time, as a needle turns when the lodestone is carried past it, not pulling, not pushing, *facing*.
