   129	Six, and no mule.
   130	
   131	He catalogued them at the rim, because it was the last halt of the year and he wanted the count. Lira with the third box and two arms. Brom with the first and the bound forearm and the good one, pricing the stumps a last time by eye, because they were the largest made thing he would ever price and he wanted the number kept. Karis with the case, and the map in the bound hand with five circles on it and a line through all five and nothing past the fifth, folded, and the notebook she had been told not to write in, open. Seln at the plain's edge with the cache, giving the count as he gave it at every halt, to the plain, flatly — "Six. Six and no mule. Nothing behind. Nothing beside." — and not saying *nothing ahead*, because for the first time in a year there was a thing ahead that was not on any line, and Seln did not count what he could not see.
   132	
   133	And Vastin. On the stick, on the twenty-ninth day of a month, with the sigil on his hand lit again a stride from the stumps and dark a stride inside them, standing with the five as he had stood with them at a fork and a counter and a cut and a shelf and a stream-bed, unadmitted, an ally who had not asked, and did not ask now, and was not asked, because the asking was a thing that would happen on a road, if there was road, and the man had learned in a season what a road was for.
   134	
   135	"West," Cael said.
   136	
   137	"West," said Brom, and picked up the box.
   138	
   139	He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.

exec
