     1	# Chapter 54 — Behind It
     2	
     3	Seln came back over the lip of the shale at first light, and he was wet to the ankle.
     4	
     5	Nobody had moved a stick. A captain had said that nobody would. Eleven people had lain all night under a step of rock, with their loads lashed and their boots on, and had watched a notch in some hills go from black to grey. Cael had not slept for the last hour of it. He had lain on his back and attended to a thing that lay along him from the heel to the palm; and it, for its part, had attended to the ground.
     6	
     7	Teague was on his feet at the edge of the flat before the small shape was fairly up. He stood as he had stood the night before, with one thumb in his belt and the other arm along its slat; and Cael was a stride behind his left shoulder, where he had been put.
     8	
     9	"You went in," said Teague.
    10	
    11	"I went in. I said that I should go no nearer than a mile, and I have gone a good deal nearer. A mile told me nothing, and I did not care to bring you nothing twice." Seln's voice was as mild as it was at any hour. "There is a pool inside the mouth, under the southern wall, where the last of some old rain lies. I went through it, since a man in water leaves no heel. I lay behind a rock at that wall's foot from the middle of the night until I could see my own hand."
    12	
    13	"And?"
    14	
    15	"It is kept."
    16	
    17	He let that stand for a breath. Then he gave the rest in order, as he gave anything.
    18	
    19	"The gap is a hundred strides from mouth to mouth, and eight wide on the floor. The floor is loose stone; it is the bed of a stream that has not run in my lifetime. On the right hand, going in, the wall is sheer, and four times my height, and nothing can be done with it. On the left it is not a wall. It is a bank of broken shale, very steep, and it goes up to a shelf of rock which runs the whole length of the gap, two thirds of the way to the top."
    20	
    21	"And they're on the shelf."
    22	
    23	"They are on the shelf. I counted nine, and I will not swear that it is not ten. There is a hollow in the back wall up there, with a fire in it which cannot be seen from any side but one, and I could not see to the back of the hollow."
    24	
    25	"Bows?"
    26	
    27	"Three. I heard three strings tried, one after another, a little before the light." He paused. "I heard a cough as well. I had heard it before, on a shelf beside a fence, from a man who has slept a cold term on stone."
    28	
    29	"So the seven are in it."
    30	
    31	"The seven, and two more, or three. They had a day's start of us, and I think now that they did not walk it to an empty place."
    32	
    33	"Faces?" said Lira.
    34	
    35	"Hoods, and cloths. In the dark, among themselves."
    36	
    37	She let her breath out through her nose, and said nothing more.
    38	
    39	"That is not the worst of it," said Seln. "There are two heaps of stone on that shelf, at its very edge. Each is as long as a cart, and as high as my chest. Each is kept from falling by a frame of timber, and the frame is kept by one wedge under its foot. I have read of such a thing; I had not seen one. A man strikes the wedge with a maul, and the heap goes down the bank onto whatever is in the bed."
    40	
    41	Nobody on that flat spoke, and Cael heard the broad man shift his stick.
    42	
    43	He had stood in a cut once, with a hillside of loose stone on either hand, and had been afraid of what lived in it. He tried now to see a heap as long as a cart let go from forty feet above a bed eight strides wide, with twelve people and a mule walking along the bottom; and he found that he could see it perfectly well, and that there was no place in the picture where anybody was standing afterward.
    44	
    45	"The first stands twenty strides inside the mouth. There is a dead pine at the shelf's lip beside it, standing, and they have roped the tree to the frame, so that the one will bring down the other. The second is sixty strides in."
    46	
    47	"And a way up?" said Teague.
    48	
    49	"One. At the far end, ninety strides in, the rock of the shelf comes down to the bed in broken steps. A man could climb it with a single hand. Both heaps stand over the ground that he must cross to reach it."
    50	
    51	"What about the bank?"
    52	
    53	"I put my palm on it. It is a slab of bare rock, as smooth as a table, and the loose stone lies upon it to the depth of my arm, like a quilt. It would not bear a child. I could not have gone a yard up it without bringing half of it down about my ears, and the whole of them to the edge to learn what had moved." He considered that, as he considered everything. "Take the quilt off, and anybody could walk up the table."
    54	
    55	Cael marked that sentence. A captain had taught him on a ridge to mark three kinds of thing in any piece of ground, and this was none of the three; it was only a thing that might be different on another day, and he put it by without knowing what it was worth.
    56	
    57	"One matter more, and I have done. They did not climb to that shelf from the floor. There is a path. It leaves the foot of the hills half a mile north of the mouth, and goes up onto the ridge, and along it. It comes down to the shelf's eastern end by a cleft, and I should suppose that it goes on down the far side to the plain. It is their road. They carried their timber by it."
    58	
    59	"How old's the timber?" said Brom.
    60	
    61	"Grey. Two cold terms, I should say, and perhaps more. It was not cut for us."
    62	
    63	"Nothing ever is," said Lira. "We only keep turning up at it."
