    68	
    69	Nobody had needed to ask him this time. He made two lines for the walls, a hand apart, and hatched the left one for the bank; and he set the shelf above the hatching, with two squares upon it for the heaps, and a cross beside the first of them for a tree. At the far end he put a row of short strokes going down, for a stair. Then he drew the path, which he had not seen, as a thread that came in over the top and ended at the shelf's eastern corner.
    70	
    71	Teague looked at it for some time.
    72	
