   261	And there had been the rings. He had stood at those oftenest of all. A child stepped into one, on the right day; and a man in grey stood by with a sheet, to write down what the child now was, and to put his name to it. He remembered a girl in a red cap. He remembered a boy who had laughed.
   262	
   263	He had told every one of them on which side of something they stood, and it had been his whole trade. He had been good at it, and scrupulous, and he had believed for the most of a long service that scruple was the same as justice.
   264	
   265	He had said what he had to say about that a few hours since, standing, in the hearing of nine people. He did not say it over. A thing that has been entered once is entered.
   266	
