/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n "lean|next|row|brass|faces the line|orientation" editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-008/EVENT-LIST.md' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
8:- **Twelve fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two; purse 127.** The eleventh (*This far?*) unused, not told to Vastin. The twelfth leans outward at made things unbidden; never turned inward by will; *still* / *set* are Cael's alone. Cleon's warning has begun to lean.
9:- Bodies: Vastin day twenty of thirty on the 299th (21, 22, 23, 24, 25 on the 304th, 26 on the 305th), Oryn's linen and two laths, stick, no kneeling, running or Wall. Brom both arms to the shoulder. Lira's arm "an arm". Karis's left palm in linen ("Write. Don't lean."). Teague's right wrist in linen under the leather (one push, on the 298th).
12:- Country, from the edition's Book 7 (chapters 47–48, 52–53, 57–58): the fork above Millrace; half a day south along the bare ridge to the Stair's bowl (the brow; the live ruts going round; the old road straight down; the grey stone and two irons numbered thirty-two, forty paces below the brow; the floor and its square hole). Beyond it the carters' road runs south and east under the ridge's foot for a carter's day, with a halting-ring; then the road swings south to a hold whose smoke shows, and an old track (two ruts, a tree in one) keeps the line for a carter's day more, goes under the turf, and comes to the rise above the Hall's marsh (reeds that stop where the line stops; stake thirty-three; the grey path; the hall in the water). A road lies a mile east of that water; two holds and a dead one on it; a fork at a heap of chock-stones (the left hand to a notch, north and west); then the road bends south and the line climbs a bare whale's back of a ridge, a mile broad, for two days, to a brow. Under the brow: a great shallow head of pale ground open to the south; old ruts high round its head; a furlong below them (a hundred and ninety paces) a fence of the registry's iron, one bar to a pace, each stamped thirty-four with the stroke and the bar; four hundred and forty yards and two strides from that line to a grey upright thing in the middle, "not one thing… there's sky in it". A carter's "day" is about half a walking crew's (chapter 47: a loaded cart takes the whole of a short day to the mill; a crew on foot is at the fork before noon).
16:1. **The calendar of the leg, laid against Book 7.** 299: dawn at the notch; the Stair's bowl a little after noon; Karis's hour; four hours on down the carters' road; camp at a beck short of the halting-ring. 300: the place where the road swings south and the old track keeps the line, at noon — **the cairn is there** (decision 2); the track through the afternoon; the rise above the Hall's marsh at dusk; camp at the track's end outside the grey stone. 301: the road a mile east of the marsh; two holds; the dead hold at dusk (dry camp one). 302: the heap of chock-stones two hours after sunrise; the road bends south before noon and the file climbs the ridge; the seep in a fold of its southern flank in the afternoon; dry camp two in a hollow on the ridge's back. 303: the ridge; the brow at dusk. That gives Hall to brow in three days, as Book 7 walked it (121st–123rd), and Stair to Hall in a day and a half of hard walking against Book 7's two and a morning at a survey's pace.
18:3. **Oryn's honest reason for walking on east** (her own rule, Book 7 chapter 53): she shut a deep wrench in Brom's left shoulder on the 298th, and "a mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it" — that is the 305th; there is a knee on her linen at day twenty-one; and there is an edge at the end of the brass twice the size of any that she has stood at, which nobody has tested, and she said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when a crew crosses one. She sent "a week, or more" up the water from Millrace with the miller's carter. At the cairn she looks up the path once, straight, and walks on; she gives the sum afterwards in three lines, as a sum and not a kindness. Nobody asks her (Cael wrote that he would not).
20:5. **Deployment.** One use in earnest: the read (Iron-adjacent), on the wet ledge at the seep on the 302nd, for the length of six breaths, paid in near sight for an hour. 32 + 1 = **33**. Nothing else is opened in the movement: not at the crest, not on the shelf, not inside the line. The twelfth's outward leans are unbidden and uncounted (the old iron at the Hall's stone; the fence; the arch; the piers; the blanket). The dry stance on the shale is a trial, not counted. **Fragments: thirteen** from the 304th.
21:6. **"Known" under each date** (the five dated entries of the leg): 299, Karis's grey stone is gone from between the two irons and a square turf has been set where it stood; 300, the hold's keeper had skins filled and priced before Brom spoke, because a rider went through two days before and said that a crew would want them; 301, both holds on the east road have their gates barred at noon, a month after the thaw; 302, nailed boots have been at the seep's pan within the week, and a hoop of a keg lies there; 303, the watch is standing to at its barricade when they top the brow.
22:7. **The Stair's bowl: the number that she already has.** The dead surveyor's "station to floor", which is also on every yearly sheet: nine lengths and a piece from the old iron at the stone to the floor's lip. She lays the kit's own cord (from Vastin's case) down the old road with Lira on its far end and Seln cutting turfs: she is checking the ruler, not the place, before she stands in front of a fence where the measure is double. It is what it was. Cael does not touch the cord (his ruling of the 168th). Teague's four stay on the brow; the tall man stands where he stood on the southern rim. Teague gives Cael a first lesson in reading ground (where two men lay on that brow for a day).
24:9. **The watch and its post (private staging).** North of the bowl a dry ravine comes down out of the hills ("the sink": the country's water goes underground). For a hundred yards it runs along the outside of the fence, and the level ground between its lip and the irons is a shelf a cart wide; then it bends inward, cuts the line, and runs to the middle, where the arch stands over it; a little beyond the arch it ends in a hole. Walking the shelf eastward: the sink's drop (three men) on the left hand, the irons on the right, the line a stride past the irons. Thirty yards in, at the shelf's narrowest, **a barricade of two timbers** on trestles, chest-high. Behind it the shelf widens to five strides: **a hut** of turf and shale, **a fire**, **kit under a hide**. At the east end the sink's lip comes to within a stride and a half of the irons (**the pinch**); beyond it a trodden **perimeter path a pace wide** goes on round the fence. **Seven hoods**, faces tied: **two crossbows**, four with shod staves, and **one low practitioner, empty-handed**, whose work on that posting is to find the line with his foot and to throw light.
25:10. **What "nobody declares" is on the page.** Seln's definition (Book 7 chapter 50): "a crack, a light"; a thing that a pair of eyes could carry to a boat. Nothing is thrown at a person and nothing is called by its name. Brom's skin under his coat and Teague's line on a timber make neither sound nor light; both are known kinds; neither is aimed at a body. Inside the line nothing is opened by anybody.
28:    - *One (the watch's).* At forty yards the practitioner declares: a white light thrown up and hung over the shelf, and a call to stand and declare. Nobody answers. Brom walks on. Two bolts: one on the breastbone, one on the left forearm, both on Iron under the coat, both turned, both paid for in bruises. The bowmen bend to span (twenty counts and more).
37:15. **The Span at noon.** Down the bowl inside the line: Cael, Karis, Lira, Brom, Seln and Vastin; Oryn at the irons with two fingers on Brom's wrist as he crosses ("Four. The same."). An arch on two piers over the sink, all of the grey, no join. No steps: the grey comes up out of the turf on either hand as a road rises, a cart wide, with no kerb, to a crown and down again. **Nothing happens on the crown.** The thirteenth listens at the grey underfoot (nothing goes into it) and the old three ribs ache where the line would come out. The view east: hills, and a notch, low and square. The piers' inner faces, reached from the sink's bed by a rib of rock, are cut in courses; Karis copies nine sheets. **The row counts one short for Cael, twice** (the Book 11 plant), and whole when Lira counts beside him; entered, ruling *hold*. Karis reads the one word that she can turn on the west pier: **[[TIER-B: the bearing runs "Through"]]**. The name: Teague has heard carters on a salt road two days north call the thing that they see from far off "the Sunken Span" (flag).
38:16. **The 304th's log:** my head line (day, place, purse 123, Vastin twenty-five); **the S19 log, verbatim, in italics, once** (it holds the rate line; its "Tomorrow" is the source's and is flagged — the office's run is three days, a little less); then my count line with the arithmetic (thirty-three; thirteen).
40:18. **The blanket (order of work; mine).** Brom's blanket, on the flat of the shelf, twenty yards from the fire and upwind; Karis, Cael, Lira, Brom and Vastin; Seln when he returns. In the middle the hide with its pinned sheet, the brass on the pencil line; round it, each turned to lie as its stone lay, north to north: one sheet from each of the three floors and the nine of the piers; at the head the flat with its six rows; at the foot her notebook open at the list, Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes; under a pebble at one corner the fair copy from Cael's coat, folded, not opened. **The rows lean along the bearing:** a sign that bears heads every run, so each run has a head and a tail; laid as they lay, every run on that blanket has its head to the west and its tail to the east, along the brass. (The twelfth sees the same thing, unbidden, and Cael looks at the brass instead.) **The point:** the two signs in the fifth place's corner are a year in the old town's count, the count in which the head of a certain skin is dated; she lays the one against the other without saying the figure until the close. **The thumbnail circle** goes round the divider's prick.
41:19. **The lamps finding (Karis's; Tier A 8, verbatim, once).** Its ground on the page: at the Stair and the Fallow and the hall, and yesterday at the irons, every sigil went at the same stride, whole, and came back whole — a Wind of the first rank, an Ember of the third, a boy, and a man who was at the top of the ladder for forty years; no dimming, no order by strength. Vastin: "If a man designed the apparatus, then the sigils —" (pinned; placed). Karis: the ruler rule (Tier B, verbatim). Two pages; the second has nothing on it. **Vastin's third "Noted."** Cael sees the foot of the second row, where a technician's sheet lies, and does not add a third page; Seln's eyes rest there once; neither speaks.
42:20. **The directive and the objection (Tier A 7).** Vastin, reading the sixth and oldest row through for the first time, finds two slips pinned inside its tape under two seals: both texts read out by him, verbatim; then his three sentences and "That's a crack." (pinned; placed). **[[TIER-B: "The Compact … Yes. Part of it."]]** He does not name the seals; nobody presses him; it is not pried.
81:1. **The ridge south.** In file from the notch's head; no speech; Vastin walking a stride behind Brom; the brow of the Stair's bowl a little after noon. Teague's four have stood on that rim. The grey stone is gone (Known).
82:2. **Nine lengths and a piece.** The cord out of the tarred case; Karis, Lira, Seln, Oryn's fingers on a wrist; an hour; it is what it was. Teague and Cael on the brow: where two men lay. The young one asks Lira what it is like.
85:5. **The track's end (300th, dusk).** The tree in the rut; the pools; the rise; the hall in its water; stake thirty-three, where he read himself twice; the twelfth leans at old iron and he counts reeds; something knocks; the log (Oryn: the cairn at noon).
97:2. **The brow at dusk.** The bowl; the fence; and a post that was not there before the snow: hut, fire, hide, timbers, seven hoods standing to. MARKER ("They knew."). Seln's line. Teague's name for the thing in the middle.
110:### Chapter 51 — Nothing on the Crown (304th)
114:4. **Down the bowl.** Oryn at the irons ("Four. The same."); the arch; the road that rises; the crown. Nothing.
115:5. **The inner faces.** Down the rib; nine sheets; a row one short, twice; MARKER ("Through").
122:4. **Lamps.** The finding; Vastin's sentence stopped; the ruler rule; two pages; "Noted."; the foot of the second row.
123:5. **The oldest row.** Two slips, two seals; the crack; MARKER; not pried.
146:- **Decision 13 changed: Oryn is not told of the thirteenth in this movement.** At the irons at noon on the 304th she sees that Cael has something and will not be told "across a row of iron"; an old man is five feet off. Lira is told first, in three words (51.1); Brom has it from her and says so behind the step (53.2). Karis and Seln have it from Lira or from the log.
147:- **Decision 18 changed: "the rows lean along the bearing"** rests on the twelfth's one unbidden glance over fourteen sheets that Karis had already laid as their walls stood, entered by her as his and as unchecked. I dropped her head-west / tail-east proof: it would have made the south wall's runs read from right to left, against the lamp's rule in the closed Deepstacks chapters (all the looking runs from the low left upward to the right).
148:- **Decision 19 changed: Seln is not at the blanket** (he has gone to look at the gap), so no glance passes between him and Cael over the second row; Cael keeps his third page alone.
149:- Vastin is not at the blanket for 52.2 (the lean is told to Karis, Lira and Brom only); Karis sends for him in 52.3.
151:- Decision 7: in the lesson on the brow the second man's want is "the old road to watch", not a rope (Teague knows nothing of the fence that Cael unbound there).
152:- 49.6 gained Lira's "three breaths" in the dark (the left foot). 51.6: Karis keeps one of the new bars from under the hide, by Teague's leave. 51.6: the log's *tomorrow* is looked at and let stand, as the truth about how near the thing has come.

